.TH SLEEPTEST 1
.SH NAME
sleeptest \- how accurate is sys->sleep
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sleeptest
[
.I ms
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sleeptest
asks for a sleep of
.I ms
milliseconds two hundred times and reports the distribution of what it
actually got: mean, minimum, median, 90th and 99th percentile, maximum,
in microseconds.
.I Ms
defaults to 16, which is the period a 60Hz frame clock asks for.
.PP
It exists because everything timed in this system stands on
.IR sys-sleep (2)
\-
.IR frameclock (2)'s
ticker, and so every game's frame pacing \- and a frame asked for every
16 milliseconds that arrives somewhere between 10 and 25 looks exactly
like choppy animation however correct the renderer is.
Nothing measured that.
.SH RESULTS
On this host, hosted
.IR emu (1)
on macOS, 60Hz:
.IP
.EX
                       mean    min median    p90    max
default               21413  16138  22013  24038  27858
no timer slack        17623  16083  17734  18104  33929
INFERNO_RT_FRAMES     16072  16024  16064  16118  16151
.EE
.PP
The first row is not slowness, it is unevenness: the answer is never
between 16 and 24 milliseconds, it is one or the other.
That is macOS coalescing timer wakeups onto an 8 millisecond quantum,
which it does to every thread that has not said otherwise, and a 60Hz
ticker under it has a period that alternates between 16 and 24.
.PP
The same numbers come out of a plain C program calling
.I nanosleep
directly, so this is the host and not
.IR emu :
.I emu
adds about half a millisecond.
.PP
.I Emu
now asks for no timer slack on every thread it makes, which is a hint
and not a reservation and so cannot starve anything.
.B INFERNO_RT_FRAMES
additionally asks for a real-time reservation \- 2 milliseconds of
computation in each 16.667 millisecond period, preemptible \- which is
what the third row measures.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sleeptest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR frameclock (2),
.IR g3probe (1)
.SH BUGS
An accurate sleep is not a steady frame, and it is not what made the
picture steady.
.PP
What did was giving the display the job of asking: a
.B CADisplayLink
drives presents, so the interval between them is the refresh interval by
construction rather than whatever the program's own clock happened to be.
Present gaps went from 13013-21174 microseconds to 16650-16682, and the
main thread stopped blocking a refresh at a time in
.BR nextDrawable .
.PP
This still matters, because the two together are what make it steady, and
because a clock that alternates 16 and 24 milliseconds is wrong on its
own terms.
.IR Castlefrankenstein (1)'s
own frames are 14.9 to 18.4 milliseconds apart now, against 6 to 28
before any of it.
.PP
Two hundred samples of one duration, with whatever else the machine is
doing at the time.
It reports a distribution rather than deciding anything, and it does not
assert.
